over the mountains of southern china a  smoke plume is the first distant sign of  this disaster  this is where china eastern flight 5735  came down  it looks less a crash site than a total  disintegration of a boeing 737 carrying  132 people there are no signs of  survivors  search and rescue workers recovered only  tiny pieces of debris  locals found bigger parts strewn further  away  the plane took off from the southern  city of kunming heading for guangzhou  two hours flight away  about an hour in cruising through clear  skies it started plummeting to the earth  this should be almost impossible in a  modern aircraft the rapid descent is  actually perplexing in some ways because  even if you have no power an airplane  will glide forward  from that height 15 minutes  so it was obviously not in a stable  flight position  guangzhou airport became the home of a  makeshift information center  we saw distressed relatives gather  my heart is very heavy because i'm a  colleague of yours  i called his relatives right after i  heard the news  because i had to tell them  this is the airport where that china  eastern airlines flight was supposed to  land and this is where relatives have  come seeking information they're behind  that screen trying to find an answer  because right now there isn't a good  answer as to why a plane that was  cruising along in the middle of the  afternoon should suddenly plunge towards  the ground  there is frankly little hope for these  relatives of finding any survivors  instead the search will turn to answers  tom cheshire sky news guangzhou
 

